[{:seon.task/name "a-run-past-its-cap-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in the related A/B (worktree a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there, round on-2, 2026-10-25, deepseek, :seon.config.agent/max-turns 30): the run of the task an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context recorded 29 screen evals and 36 model evals, no :seon.end fact, status :running and its agent :busy for over an hour after its last turn, with an empty stderr (runs/store-agents/main/a1.err, 0 bytes); its root answered every other request. The round's stats are docs/vector.md; the store was erased with the round (an experiment store), so what the agent was evaluating is not kept. Not known: whether the last eval hung (a form with no bound; every form has :seon.config.agent/form-ticks now), the model call never answered (seon.model's edge, no timeout by design: a never-answered call is a recorded stall: docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs), or the loop missed its cap. The first step reproduces it with the scripted provider at the cap (a reply whose eval never returns; a call never answered) and reads the agent's state over the server while it is busy (seon_agents, the agent's :fx-pending, its last eval record), so the stall is a fact on the run (blocked, stalled) and the run ends rather than holding its agent: never a clock deciding; the fact names what it waits on. Seen again 2026-11-14 (context, random round 1): agent o8c24b62c51eec13 (my.agent/a-functions-answer-is-not-an-evals, deepseek) answered its turn-24 evals at 09:07:42, the last of them a seon.repl/edit! of seon.repl/response (the function that builds every eval's answer) in its own process; then no model call and no eval for over 35 minutes, :seon.agent/status :busy, its process (pid 2552) at 0% CPU with every thread waiting, and no stall recorded under runs/stalls. Evidence (samples, lsof): .worktrees/context/target/evidence-o8c24b62/. Not known: whether its own edit broke its turn path (the hypothesis) or the next model call was never answered; reading the turn loop's pending wait (the agent's :fx-pending) settles it. Two causes, both the system's, not the task's: (1) the turn loop waits with no end after the agent redefines what its own turn path calls; it must answer a typed error, and the turn machinery must run from the program, never from the REPL's redefinition, so an agent may redefine anything (docs/plan.md Stage 4.3, REPL redefinitions); (2) nothing observed the stall: seon.template/stall's task never fired for o8c24b62c51eec13 (no stall task in the store, none under runs/stalls), which is what let a hang sit unseen. The same class in root's own process (2026-11-14, context, plan Stage 0.6): a seon_eval at root load-file'd a branch's uncommitted src/seon/source.clj into the standing root, so for about two minutes root ran a task-file-text main's program does not hold (a changed signature), found only because the session said so and loaded main's file back. Root's process must say when it runs a definition its main program does not hold: deps' jolt.host/*on-root-change* (landed 2026-11-14) can note each var-root change, and a definition that differs from main's recorded form is shown as root's problem, so the next one is visible without anyone confessing. The same class from a landing (2026-11-14 13:15, context): agent nae67b62c8f6a952 was live when 1916c1b31c landed; its seon_done's hand-back! called my.branch/merge-main!, which loaded the landed seon.turn/hand-back-end into the running process, and the old hand-back! body, still running, called the new function with the old argument: a contract error in place of the run's end. A landing's code must not change a run already under way: the turn path runs from the program it began on, and new code takes effect at its next start. Design (context, 2026-11-14; root approved as context's #1 and #2; building waits on root's read of this design). The hook, agreed with runtime 14:35: runtime binds jolt.host/on-root-change around each REPL eval (seon.repl) and each seon.program/load-form! (landings, catch-ups) and gives the agent's run one message per eval or load, {:seon.program/roots-changed #{var syms} :seon.program/by <eval handle | commit>}, with a schema entry; that is all runtime supplies. The cause, read at both ends [M]: Jolt reads a var's root with no per-thread override (reference-code/jolt host/chez/rt.ss var-root-set!, vars.ss jolt-var-get), so one process holds one program at a time and every call of the turn path goes through the roots the REPL last wrote. So a run keeps two maps, sized by what changed, never by the program: :run, each changed var's root as the run began (taken at its first change, O(d)), and :repl, its root as the REPL or a merge left it. The turn's own code (observe, the status, the draw, the model call, seon.repl/response and the eval's record) runs with :run installed; seon.repl/eval-string installs :repl around the agent's forms alone (the handler's eval) and :run after them, so the agent's code runs its own program and the loop the one it began on. A merge-main! in a run (a landing's catch-up) changes :repl the same way, so a landing never changes a run under way (the hand-back! case). A test child installs :repl at its start (the fork copies both maps), so proofs test the branch's program. end! installs :repl and clears both: new code takes effect at the next start. An eval that changed a var the run holds says so in its answer (saved on the branch; the run takes it at its next start). Limit, said in docs/turn.md: the agent's own background threads read :run while the loop runs. Weighed and not taken: the loop in root over the agent's REPL, as an outside agent's (it moves the problem into root's process, where landings load, and the draw reads the agent's page walk); a copy of the turn path compiled per run (O(N) forms at ~2.5M ticks each). Estimate: two root writes per changed var per eval, d ≤ 10: under 10k ticks an eval; nothing when nothing changed. (2) A stall is seen: [M] seon.agent-proc's stall check exempts an agent with an effect outstanding ((zero? fx-out), agent_proc.clj:123) and fx! :model waits with no bound (seon.agent/fx!, await-reply with no ms), so a model call never answered, or a turn path that hung inside an effect's wait, records nothing. First step [H to settle]: read whether the model effect's stream reaches the agent proc as frames; then the check counts quiet from the last frame or the outstanding effect's last progress, so a call that streams nothing past :seon.config.agent/stall-ms is a stall and seon.system/stall-task fires, with its evidence, never an end. Proofs, red first: seon.turn-test/a-run-keeps-the-program-it-began-on (an eval in a run redefines seon.repl/response to hang; the next turn is drawn and answered by the run's own, and the REPL holds the redefinition), seon.turn-test/a-landing-under-a-run-changes-nothing-until-its-next-start, seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what (a scripted call never answered is a recorded stall). Agreed 2026-11-14 (context 3, with root, runtime, deps-2, tests-2). Root's read: capturing the loop's functions at run start cannot hold [M by source]: an app's call of a function the build emitted is direct-linked to its Scheme binding (Jolt backend_scheme.clj emit-invoke), which var-root-set! writes through (rt.ss), so a captured work! still calls an agent's new seon.repl/response. Root's conditions on the swap: it lives in one place, seon.repl/eval-string's try/finally (Jolt's one dynamic-wind), so no escape leaves the REPL's roots under the loop; both maps are the run's own state, no new atom; before it is built, deps-2 gives the cost of a per-thread root overlay (it must cover direct-linked bindings too), and if near zero the fix is Jolt's and the swap goes. Runtime builds the hook (on-root-change around each eval and load-form!, one :seon.program/roots-changed message) on root's pick; deps-2's next pin passes the old root, (f var old), which replaces the run-start snapshot. A proc's step fn swapped by a def is runtime's (:proc-swap, seon.system/a-landing-restarts-root-only-for-a-binary-change). The stall: the model effect's declared bound, fx! passing it to await-reply, and the stall counted from an effect's progress are runtime's (seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error), never a wider stall check; the turn's part is reading :seon.bound/reached or :seon.agent/stalled-wait as a turn the next one reads, recorded as the stall. Root's pick, 2026-11-14 15:30: no swap. Deps' per-run routing, jolt.host/a-runs-redefinition-is-its-threads-alone (jolt/deps 1493f66bba): a var a live run redefines gets a dispatcher as its linked binding, the run's threads seeing the root as of the run's first write and threads with jolt.host/*run* nil the new root (measured by deps: +~3 ns on overlaid vars only, 0 elsewhere; an overlay on every call, +0.8 ns and no inlining, refused). Context's part: seon.turn/work! and hand-back! take (jolt.host/run-begin!) and bind jolt.host/*run* around the run; seon.repl/eval-string binds it nil around the agent's forms; seon.turn/end! calls jolt.host/run-end! in its finally. Then the proof seon.turn-test/a-run-keeps-the-program-it-began-on (kept red at 891a3e7e1e) comes back and goes green. Until then the stall's turn part and seon.ctx.wire/a-turn-is-a-component-of-the-request go first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-run-keeps-the-program-it-began-on seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what seon.turn-test/a-landing-under-a-run-changes-nothing-until-its-next-start}
  :seon.task/needs #{"jolt.host/a-runs-redefinition-is-its-threads-alone"}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-breaks-its-own-render-is-told-and-goes-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 by the context lab's curation pilot (a scripted replay of task 618a903ed3 on a reverse-patched tree; repro in /private/tmp/seon-l2r/618a903ed3/repro-no-end/README.md): turn 3 made three calls; edit! of seon.ctx/members-map was refused, the next call's edit! of seon.ctx/join applied anyway (join now calls (members-map pairs flag)), and the agent's next context render threw; its following turn threw the same way and the run ended :no-end, \"two turns in a row threw\" (seon.turn/work!). The first cause found then, that a call answering an error value did not stop the reply's later calls, is superseded: `seon.repl/run-calls` now runs every call of a reply whatever came before it, so one failure does not cost the reply's other evals, and each call's result reads in order after the one before. What stands: an agent that breaks code its own turn loop runs (seon.ctx, the render: Seon improves itself) can no longer be sent anything, since the request is drawn by that code. A turn that threw is told, as far as it can be (`seon.turn/turn-error!` writes the error as a message from seon on its branch, which the next turn's request reads), but when the request itself cannot be drawn there is no model turn to read the message, and work! ends the run :error on the second throw in a row. Correct: a turn whose request cannot be drawn sends the previous request as it was sent (the prefix the cache already holds, seon.ctx.wire/request) with the error as one message printed by the one printer, so the agent reads why and fixes its branch; the run ends only by the existing caps (max-turns), never because the agent's own code broke its page. Owner's ruling 2026-10-28 (never crash, never hang: errors go to whoever can fix them). Design to be shown to the owner before it is built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-page-cannot-be-drawn-sends-its-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-asks-whether-a-test-is-a-process-test"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: (seon.agent/branch-graph) at the REPL is an arity error (it takes db, base db and inputs), and no public my.agent function answers \"is this test a process test on my branch\", which decides whether (my.agent/prove) runs it or defers it. Add that answer to my.agent beside tests-reaching and deferred (status of a test: :process, :plain), reading the reach graph the test plan reads. Proof: (my.agent/tests-reaching ..) results carry :process? for a test reaching a process var. An attempt (a Seon agent's merge on a curated root, 2026-11-10, 67893381bb) made my.agent/tests-reaching answer {:test :process?} from (:process (agent/test-plan ...)); Tests' review: NOT done. Its proof seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests is red ({false 2}): the scratch test calling seon.test-store/binary-sh is not flagged :process?, so the gap is upstream, why a scratch namespace's form using a process var gets no :process? in the branch graph (the form's process-var use not analyzed, or judges?/graph-index treating scratch namespaces differently). And test-plan digests every test (~165M ticks) to read a few: the cheap form indexes only the tests found, (program/graph-index g in (zipmap (map str ts) (map vector ts))). And the proof was green in its shard of two proves and red by name: it depends on state it does not make (the process store's), which the next attempt's proof must make itself."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-turn-is-a-turn-with-only-the-merge-left"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (2026-11-10, Context, a test root from jolt/request-launch, store .worktrees/request-launch/runs/store): request/add-a-function-demo-536871029's agent never named a proof; two siblings merged demo.math/triple first, so its status held an unmerged entry; after merging main, root closed it held 'ended merge-failed (3 turns without green > :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns 2): no-proofs'. Cause: seon.turn counts a merge turn on every turn of a merging agent while any unmerged entry is open, whatever else the done rule says, so the task's own work counted as merging. Plan: a turn counts as a merge turn only when the done rule's reasons, minus :unmerged, are empty (the unmerged entries are all that stands between the task and green); any other open reason is the task's own work, counted against :seon.config.agent/max-turns only, and the agent is told its first reason. It deletes the whole?/merge-step? case in the count: a merge task's only reason is its unmerged entries. Proof, red first: a task with :no-proofs and an unmerged entry runs past max-merge-turns turns and is not closed merge-failed; once its proof is named and green, its next turns with only :unmerged left count, and past the limit it is closed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-calls-seon-makes-for-an-agent-are-stored-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11: 'I just want it to be deterministic and to work'. What exists: at each request the render makes again the calls Seon makes for the agent (its ctx at the first point, the page, the diff between two points), so a change to a render's code moves the bytes of old turns in the request, and a finished agent whose own render drew part of its page cannot be shown once its process is gone. Plan: seon.turn writes each such call at the turn's open as an eval with the attributes an agent's own eval has (its form, its answer, its basis), `:seon.eval/by` the system's entity, its intent and message read from the metadata of the function called; the request and the page read the stored answer (docs/ctx.md#what-the-model-reads-and-what-the-page-renders). No new attribute. An example session then keeps them too: they are what the model read at that turn, where today an example keeps the model's evals and drops the harness's (seon.ctx.session/session-tx). Deletes: the render-time calls in seon.ctx.wire and their second producer on the page. Cost: one write a turn, of the diff's size. Proof, red first: the same store gives the same request bytes before and after a render's code changes; a compaction and a wiped process included; a recorded request is equal byte for byte. Measured 2026-11-11 (Optimization, the stored profile of seon.render-cost-test/a-cold-request-draws-what-the-model-reads, 74k calls): seon.print.select is 36% of a cold request's calls (each record's eval value printed again at every request) and seon.ratom 33% (a view and a watcher per deref in a request nothing watches after). So the stored eval keeps its printed text, made once at its turn, and a request reads it; and a cold request reads untracked."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turns-own-calls-are-stored-and-read-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-loops-own-eval-is-not-sent-to-the-model"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Context. Found 2026-11-12 by Web, in one real agent turn on jolt/debuglabel's own system (root's session, deepseek). The turn loop's own eval, (seon.turn/work! {:task \"request/root\" :max-turns 40}), was sent to the model as a user message {:by \"root\" :code ...}: root is both the agent and the author of that eval, so the rule that sends an eval written by the agent itself sends it. The model then spent a whole call replying that its turn was done, and work! ran a second time (:max-turns 50) with that reply. The ordinary way: an eval's origin, recorded when it is made, says whether the model wrote it; the request reads that, not the author. Plan: the request leaves out an eval whose origin is the turn loop's own, whoever wrote it; the page folds it with the system's other evals. Proof to write, beside seon.ctx.wire-test's eval-placement tests: root's request after one turn holds no work! eval, and one request makes one call for a one-eval answer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-resume-costs-its-branch-and-context-not-gigabytes"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (context's real-task loop, evidence/context-loop-2026-11-12-1620.md): a paid agent's resume (``seon.turn/work!`` after its process was restarted) cost 138 s, 2.7e9 ticks and 21 GB allocated. Lower bound: a resume reads its branch's head and builds its context once, a few MB and under a second (a fresh agent's start measures 45-75 ms). 21 GB is over three orders of magnitude above that, so it is the finding: trace work!'s fns (``seon.profile/measure!``'s per-var ticks and bytes, the eval's :fns) on a resumed agent and name what it reads or rebuilds whole (a walk of its whole history, a render of every turn, a re-index). Proof, red first: a resumed agent's first work! allocates within 10x of a fresh agent's first turn. See docs/cost.md#what-is-measured."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-resume-costs-its-branch-and-context-not-gigabytes}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-loops-decision-is-a-pure-step", :seon.task/plan "Found by tests and context, 2026-11-13: seon.turn/work! decides each turn (go on, end :error after two throws in a row before a model call, reset the count when the model answered, stop at the cap even when the status is broken, end :green when the done rule has no reason) inside a loop that also does the effects (begin!, observe, status-now, act!, end!, tell-error!, agent/heard-main, my.branch/merge-main!). So its rules are tested only by with-redefs on those eight, as seon.agent-test/a-turn-that-throws-is-the-agents-to-read-and-the-second-ends-its-work does. The loop's decision becomes a pure (next-state st turn-outcome) -> {:st :next|:end ...}, as a Flow step fn is (docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows), and work! a reduce of it over the outcomes its effects answer. Owner: context (seon.turn is the loop). Proof: the throws test, moved onto next-state with no redefs.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/the-loops-decision-is-a-pure-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-status-is-computed-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by context's duplication audit, 2026-11-14 (the owner: no duplicated effort; plan item 7), both ends read [M]. How the work stands is computed twice on a green turn: seon.turn/status-now answers the page component's kept status (seon.render/kept-of) and otherwise calls seon.ctx.status/status itself, and seon.turn/judged calls status-now again after the judge; a component that threw is recomputed by the fallback and throws again, labelled seon.turn/status-now. Each pass does delta-changes, caused-split and commit-as-db (up to ~15M ticks cold [H]). Change: the turn reads the kept status only; a component that threw is the turn's error (turn-error!), never a recompute; judged reads the kept status after the judge's facts move it. Deletes: status-now's fallback call. Proof: a green turn calls seon.ctx.status/status once (a cost proof's calls)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turns-status-is-computed-once}}]
